/-! # Fixed groups for torus automorphisms over a scheme

Source: Conrad, Reductive group schemes, Lemma 2.2.4, Example 2.2.6 and the proof of
Corollary 3.3.5, printed pp.65--67, 101
(blueprint: lem-sfb-relative-torus-automorphism-fixed-group).
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory MonoidalCategory CartesianMonoidalCategory
open scoped MonObj

namespace AlgebraicGeometry.GroupScheme

/-- Points fixed after every test-scheme extension, for a given action morphism.
No reducedness or flatness restriction is placed on the test schemes.
Source: Conrad, Definition 2.2.1 and Example 2.2.6, printed pp.64, 67
(blueprint: lem-sfb-relative-torus-automorphism-fixed-group). -/
def fixedPoints {S : Scheme.{u}} {R H : Over S} (a : R ⊗ H ⟶ H)
    (U : Over S) : Set (U ⟶ H) :=
  {x | ∀ (V : Over S) (f : V ⟶ U) (r : V ⟶ R),
    lift r (f ≫ x) ≫ a = f ≫ x}

/-- The canonical action morphism after base change: first identify the product of
pullbacks with the pullback of the product, then pull back the action morphism.
Source: Conrad, functorial centralizers, Definition 2.2.1, printed p.64
(blueprint: lem-sfb-relative-torus-automorphism-fixed-group). -/
def actionBaseChange {S T : Scheme.{u}} (f : T ⟶ S) {R H : Over S}
    (a : R ⊗ H ⟶ H) :
    (Over.pullback f).obj R ⊗ (Over.pullback f).obj H ⟶ (Over.pullback f).obj H :=
  Functor.LaxMonoidal.μ (Over.pullback f) R H ≫ (Over.pullback f).map a

/-- The fixed functor for a torus acting by group automorphisms on a smooth affine
finitely presented group with connected geometric fibres is a smooth affine closed
subgroup of finite presentation with connected geometric fibres. The same inclusion
represents the fixed functor after every base change. Reductivity is preserved.
Source: Conrad, Lemma 2.2.4, Example 2.2.6, paragraph before Remark 3.1.5 and
Corollary 3.3.5 proof, printed pp.65--67, 83, 101
(blueprint: lem-sfb-relative-torus-automorphism-fixed-group).

Proof:
1. First work over an affine base $U=\operatorname{Spec}A$ where $R=\mathbf G_m^r$. Form
   $E=H\rtimes R$, whose underlying scheme is $H\times_U R$, with
   $(h,t)(h',t')=(h\,a(t,h'),tt')$ and inverse $(a(t^{-1},h^{-1}),t^{-1})$. The action
   identities prove the group diagrams. The subgroup $j:R\to E$ is $t\mapsto(1,t)$. It is
   closed: on affine coordinates its ideal is generated by $b-e_H^*(b)$ for generators $b$ of
   the coordinate algebra of $H$. By [lem-weil-pbc-fibre-product-affine-spectra], $E$ is affine.
   By [lem-weil-pbc-lfp-affine-opens-finite-presentation], write the coordinate algebra of $H$
   with finitely many generators and relations. Adjoining $z_i,w_i$ and relations $z_iw_i-1$
   presents the algebra of $E$, so $E$ is finitely presented. It is smooth by
   [def-sbc-smooth-morphisms]: it is locally finitely presented, and in a square-zero lifting
   problem the $H$-coordinate lifts by smoothness of $H$, while each unit coordinate of $R$
   lifts to a unit (a lift of an inverse has product $1+$ a square-zero element). Thus all
   hypotheses of the relative split-torus centralizer theorem hold.
2. Apply [lem-sfb-relative-torus-centralizer-normalizer] to $j:R\hookrightarrow E$. Its
   centralizer $C$ is smooth affine of finite presentation, closed in $E$, and commutes with
   every base change. On every test scheme, $(h,t)(1,u)=(h,tu)$ whereas
   $(1,u)(h,t)=(a(u,h),ut)$. Since $R$ is commutative, $(h,t)$ centralizes $R$ exactly when $h$
   is fixed by the full $R$-action after every further test-scheme extension. Let
   $F=C\times_{R}U$ using the projection to $R$ and its identity section. Then $F$ represents
   $H^R$, is closed in $H$, and the maps $(h,t)\mapsto((h,1),t)$ and $((h,1),t)\mapsto(h,t)$
   give inverse scheme isomorphisms $C\simeq F\times_U R$. The group law on $C$ becomes the
   direct-product law because $R$ acts trivially on $F$.
3. The affine coordinate algebra $B$ of $C$ is finitely presented over $A$ by
   [lem-weil-pbc-lfp-affine-opens-finite-presentation]. The algebra of $F$ is
   $B/(z_1-1,\ldots,z_r-1)$, so [lem-weil-pbc-finite-presentation-quotient-fg-ideal] makes $F$
   finitely presented. It is smooth directly from [def-sbc-smooth-morphisms]: for any
   square-zero affine lifting problem into $F$, compose with $F\hookrightarrow C$, lift using
   the smoothness of $C$, and then compose with the morphism $C\simeq F\times R\to F$. This
   composite restricts to the original point because the projection is a retraction. Thus no
   invalid inference of smoothness from a closed immersion is used.
4. These local representing functors have canonical compatible identifications on all overlaps,
   even for different splittings. The fixed condition is fpqc local on a test scheme: a section
   of $H$ glues uniquely by [lem-weil-pbc-fpqc-representable-descent]; after any $V\to W$ and
   $u\in R(V)$, pull back the covering by [lem-galois-fpqc-covering-base-change]. The two
   sections $a(u,h_V)$ and $h_V$ agree on that cover, hence agree on $V$ by the uniqueness
   assertion of the same descent node. Conversely any fixed section restricts to fixed sections.
   Therefore the fixed subfunctor of $H$ is an fpqc sheaf, and its locally represented
   identifications satisfy the cocycle condition literally, by equality of subfunctors of $H$.
5. For a general affine $U\subset S$, take an fppf splitting cover of $R_U$ and refine it by
   finitely many affine opens using [lem-pc-fppf-affine-refinement]. Their disjoint union $U'\to
   U$ is a singleton fpqc cover: its source is affine by
   [lem-weil-pbc-affine-morphism-permanence](4), it is flat and surjective by the definition of
   the fppf cover, and it satisfies the fpqc covering condition by
   [lem-trf-fppf-covering-is-fpqc]. The disjoint union $F'\to U'$ of the groups constructed in
   steps 1--3 is affine and has its canonical descent datum from step 4. The finite coproducts
   used here exist by [lem-weil-pbc-scheme-coproduct-family]. Apply
   [lem-galois-fpqc-affine-descent-effective] to obtain $F_U$. Descend its map to $H_U$ by
   [lem-galois-fpqc-morphisms-descend]. Step 4 identifies its represented functor with
   $H_U^{R_U}$.
6. The map $F_U\to H_U$ is a closed immersion by [lem-pc-fpqc-descend-closed-immersion], using
   the pulled-back fpqc cover of $H_U$ from [lem-galois-fpqc-covering-base-change]. Affineness
   descends by [lem-weil-pbc-fpqc-descend-affine], and local finite presentation by
   [lem-weil-pbc-fpqc-descend-locally-finite-presentation]. An affine morphism is quasi-compact
   and quasi-separated by [lem-weil-pbc-affine-morphism-permanence], so $F_U\to U$ is of finite
   presentation. Smoothness descends by [lem-trf-smooth-fpqc-base-descent]: the finite disjoint
   union $U'$ of affine schemes is affine, its map to affine $U$ is quasi-compact, flat and
   surjective. The affine groups $F_U$ on affine opens of $S$ have canonical overlap
   isomorphisms since they represent the same functor. These are gluing data;
   [lem-weil-pbc-glue-schemes] gives a global $F$ representing $H^R$. Its map to $H$ is a closed
   immersion by [lem-pc-fpqc-descend-closed-immersion] for the Zariski cover of $H$. Affineness
   and local finite presentation follow from the same descent nodes for the Zariski cover of
   $S$. Smoothness follows from [lem-weil-pbc-smooth-restrict-open-subschemes](b) and
   [lem-weil-pbc-smooth-local-on-source]. Thus $F$ is smooth affine of finite presentation over
   $S$.
7. Fixed points form a subgroup on every test scheme: $a(u,hh')=a(u,h)a(u,h')=hh'$ and
   $a(u,h^{-1})=a(u,h)^{-1}=h^{-1}$, and the identity is fixed. These natural operations give
   the group operations on $F$ by its representing property; their group identities follow from
   those on $H$. For any $S'\to S$ and any $S'$-scheme $W$, being fixed in $H_{S'}(W)$ by
   $R_{S'}$ after every $W$-scheme extension is exactly the original condition for $H(W)$ and
   $R$. Consequently $F\times_S S'$ represents $(H_{S'})^{R_{S'}}$, canonically and compatibly
   with its inclusion in $H_{S'}$. This proves arbitrary base change, including nonflat and
   nonreduced base changes.
8. It remains to check the geometric fibres. Fix a geometric point with algebraically closed
   field $k$. An fppf splitting cover for $R_k$ has a nonempty affine finite-type open over $k$;
   a closed point of it is $k$-rational by [lem-wf-affine-nullstellensatz-vanishing](1). Pulling
   the splitting back to this point gives $R_k\simeq\mathbf G_m^r$. The torus is smooth by the
   unit-lifting calculation of step 1. It is geometrically connected: after any field extension
   its coordinate ring is a Laurent polynomial domain, so its spectrum is irreducible (two
   nonempty basic opens intersect because the product of two nonzero elements is nonzero). Apply
   [lem-weil-pbc-geometrically-connected-product-components] with $X=R_k$ and $Y=H_k$. Since
   $H_k$ is connected by hypothesis, $E_k=H_k\times R_k$ is connected. Its identity is a
   $k$-point, so [lem-sbc-connected-rational-point-geometrically-connected] makes it
   geometrically connected. It is smooth by step 1.
9. Apply [lem-sfb-torus-centralizer-smooth-connected] to the smooth geometrically connected
   affine $k$-group $E_k$ and its embedded torus $R_k$. The centralizer $C_k$ is connected. The
   isomorphism of step 2, valid on this fibre by step 7, is $C_k\simeq F_k\times R_k$.
   Projection to $F_k$ is surjective, since it has the section $f\mapsto(f,1)$. Its continuous
   image is connected, so $F_k$ is connected. This proves connected geometric fibres without
   assuming that the original action was inner or faithful.
10. Suppose now $H$ is reductive. Over the fixed algebraically closed field $k$, let
   $V=R_u(E_k)$ be the smooth connected closed normal unipotent radical provided by
   [lem-sfb-unipotent-radical-existence]. The projection $V\to R_k$ is trivial by
   [lem-sfb-unipotent-to-multiplicative-trivial], so $V$ factors through the scheme-theoretic
   kernel $H_k$ of $E_k\to R_k$. It is a smooth connected closed normal unipotent subgroup of
   $H_k$: closedness follows from its closed immersion in $E_k$, and normality follows by
   restricting conjugation from $E_k$. Reductivity of $H_k$ gives $V=1$. Every smooth connected
   closed normal unipotent subgroup of $E_k$ is contained in $V$ by the cited radical theorem.
   Hence $E_k$ is reductive.
11. By [lem-sfb-reductive-torus-fixed-subgroup], the centralizer $C_{E_k}(R_k)$ is reductive,
   since step 10 proved $E_k$ reductive and $R_k$ is an embedded torus. Under $C_k\simeq
   F_k\times R_k$, the factor $F_k$ is a closed normal subgroup (the kernel of projection to
   $R_k$). It is smooth by steps 3--6 and smooth base change [lem-pc-proper-base-change](2), and
   connected by step 9. Thus [lem-sfb-connected-normal-subgroup-reductive] applies and proves
   $F_k$ reductive. Since this holds for every geometric fibre, the already smooth affine
   finitely presented group $F$ is reductive by the definition in the item. Rank zero and the
   trivial action are included: the formulas give $F=H$ and $C=H\times R$ in those cases.
-/
theorem exists_torus_automorphism_fixed_group {S : Scheme.{u}}
    (H R : Grp (Over S))
    [Smooth H.X.hom] [IsAffineHom H.X.hom]
    [LocallyOfFinitePresentation H.X.hom] [QuasiCompact H.X.hom]
    [QuasiSeparated H.X.hom] [GeometricallyConnected H.X.hom]
    (hR : IsRelativeTorus R.X) [ModObj R.X H.X]
    (ha : ∀ (W : Over S) (r : W ⟶ R.X) (x y : W ⟶ H.X),
      r • (x * y) = (r • x) * (r • y)) :
    ∃ (F : Grp (Over S)) (i : F ⟶ H),
      IsClosedImmersion i.hom.hom.left ∧
      Smooth F.X.hom ∧ IsAffineHom F.X.hom ∧
      LocallyOfFinitePresentation F.X.hom ∧ QuasiCompact F.X.hom ∧
      QuasiSeparated F.X.hom ∧ GeometricallyConnected F.X.hom ∧
      (∀ U : Over S,
        Set.range (fun z : U ⟶ F.X ↦ z ≫ i.hom.hom) = fixedPoints γ[R.X, H.X] U) ∧
      (∀ (T : Scheme.{u}) (f : T ⟶ S) (U : Over T),
        Set.range (fun z : U ⟶ ((Over.pullback f).mapGrp.obj F).X ↦
          z ≫ ((Over.pullback f).mapGrp.map i).hom.hom) =
            fixedPoints (actionBaseChange f γ[R.X, H.X]) U) ∧
      (IsReductive H → IsReductive F) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry.GroupScheme
